 ### Single-Pass Voxelization
-A voxelization approach that utilizes the geometry shader to reduce draw calls by selecting the axis from which each triangle has the largest overall projection for fragment generation [S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of multiple draw calls in multi-pass approaches. It differs from standard rasterization by using conservative rasterization to generate fragments as long as there is something inside the pixel, not only if its center is covered [S6, unofficial]. Hardware support for conservative rasterization is available on Nvidia Maxwell architecture and later; RTX driver support is available for Turing and Pascal architecture GPUs [S6, unofficial]. A pitfall is that GPUs only generate fragments if a pixel's center is covered by a triangle, leading to small cracks at the edges of triangles, and conservative rasterization may incorrectly fill empty cells [S6, unofficial].
+A voxelization approach that utilizes the geometry shader to reduce draw calls by selecting the axis from which each triangle has the largest overall projection for fragment generation [S6, unofficial]. It solves the problem of multiple draw calls for objects with the same material. It employs conservative rasterization to generate fragments as long as there is something inside the pixel, not only if its center is covered [S6, unofficial]. It uses an MSAA render target to solve the issue of incorrectly filling empty cells caused by conservative rasterization generating too many fragments [S6, unofficial]. Hardware support for conservative rasterization is available on Nvidia Maxwell architecture and later; RTX driver support is available for Turing and Pascal architecture GPUs [S6, unofficial]. A pitfall is that GPUs only generate fragments if a pixel's center is covered by a triangle, leading to small cracks at the edges of triangles, and conservative rasterization may incorrectly fill cells that should be empty [S6, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of triangles and the number of voxels covered by those triangles [S1][S5, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Linear with respect to the number of occupied voxels when using sparse hash structures [S3, S4, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** GPU-based variants offer significant speedups (e.g., 50x) over CPU implementations for high-resolution grids [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Fast generation of surface voxel shells; leverages existing graphics hardware (GPU) or efficient CPU rasterization algorithms [S1][S4, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Output mesh size can be very large compared to the input mesh (e.g., 100x increase in vertices) [S2, unofficial]. Standard rasterization may produce holes if precision is not handled [S2, S6, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When a surface representation (shell) is needed for voxel art or collision detection, and performance is critical [S2, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When deep volumetric data (interior density) is required without additional filling steps, or when memory bandwidth is severely constrained by the output mesh size [S2, S5, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot B)$, where $N$ is the number of triangles and $B$ is the average number of voxels in the bounding box of a triangle [S1][S4, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(V)$, where $V$ is the number of unique occupied voxels [S3, S4, unofficial]. Using a hash table makes it efficient for sparse meshes compared to dense 3D arrays [S3, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** GPU-based methods offer significant speedups (e.g., 50x) for high-resolution grids compared to CPU-based nested loops [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Fast generation of surface-level voxel data; leverages existing graphics hardware; simple implementation [S1][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Output mesh has significantly more vertices and faces than the input, scaling with voxel density, which can impact rendering performance [S2, unofficial]. Standard rasterization can produce holes if triangle normals are parallel to the view direction or if conservative rasterization is not used [S6, unofficial].
+*   **When to use:** When surface representation is sufficient (shell voxelization) and real-time or high-throughput generation is required [S2, S5, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** When deep interior voxel data is required or when exact geometric intersection without rasterization artifacts is critical [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from the need to convert polygonal models into voxel formats for real-time rendering and volume processing. The Point-Cloud-Utils library popularized the Python-based rasterization approach [S1]. The C-based voxelizer library demonstrated the generation of cube meshes from rasterized voxels [S4, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in voxel art tools, collision detection systems, and as a preprocessing step for ray-traced voxel global illumination [S2, S6, unofficial].
+Originating from the need to convert polygonal models for volume rendering and voxel art, this method leverages the efficiency of rasterization pipelines [S1][S2, unofficial]. The Point-Cloud-Utils library popularized a Python-based implementation using scanline rasterization [S1]. The C library `voxelizer` demonstrated a hash-based approach for generating cube meshes [S4, unofficial]. The technique is widely used in tools like GeeXLab for real-time visualization [S2, unofficial] and in academic research for real-time ray-traced voxel global illumination [S6, unofficial].
 
